<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN>

<PLUGIN
  name="fanctrlplus2"
  author="ck9393 / andrebrait"
  version="2026.11.12"
  launch="Settings/fanctrlplus2"
  pluginURL="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/main/plugin/fanctrlplus2.plg"
  min="6.9.0"
  support="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/issues"
  project="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus"
  readme="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus#readme"
>

<ICON>
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/images/fanctrlplus2.png
</ICON>

<CHANGES>
<![CDATA[
### 2026.11.12

## Fewer false control-loss warnings at full speed

When a fan is set to full speed, some controllers report it as mode 0 instead of manual mode 1. This includes the nct6775 driver family, used by many Nuvoton boards. FanCtrl Plus 2 no longer treats this as a loss of control while full speed is both the requested and the read-back value. A fan that stops, a controller switched to automatic mode, or one that refuses to leave full speed still triggers a warning.

The fan curve tooltip now names the disk group, as the current-reading marker already does. The plugin changelog shows stable releases only, and support diagnostics now record the exact test-build version.

This is software-based fan control, not independent thermal protection. Monitor actual fan speeds and temperatures. Back up your settings before updating. If rolling back, reinstall the older plugin manifest and restore your backup; do not uninstall, because uninstalling deletes the configuration.


### 2026.11.10z

## Fan alerts and support diagnostics

Fan speed mismatch warnings now restart their waiting period when the requested PWM changes, reducing false warnings while a controller or fan catches up. The controller's readback must differ from the requested speed by 10 or more percentage points, including for MSI nct6687 system-fan warnings. When a substantial fan RPM response is observed, a warning can include it as supporting evidence; this does not prove which controller changed the fan. Other problem alerts remain active.

A new "Download diagnostics" button in General Settings creates a ZIP containing anonymized fan settings, hardware readings, and structured fan-control events. It excludes raw logs, disk identifiers, fan and sensor names, and custom sensor scripts. Hardware characteristics can still identify a machine, so review the archive before sharing it.

This is software-based fan control, not independent thermal protection. Monitor actual fan speeds and temperatures. Back up your settings before updating. If rolling back, reinstall the older plugin manifest and restore your backup; do not uninstall, because uninstalling deletes the configuration.

### 2026.11.08

## Editable fan curves

Configure each disk group, CPU temperature source, and auxiliary temperature source with a 2–8 point fan curve. Choose linear interpolation or stepped control, set cooling hysteresis, and configure an optional emergency temperature and speed that can exceed the normal output cap. Curve points retain their absolute speeds when the cap changes.

Disk groups are optional, so a fan can use CPU or auxiliary temperature without assigning a disk. Existing valid settings are preserved as equivalent two-point curves; export a configuration backup before updating.

This is software-based control, not independent thermal protection. Monitor actual fan speeds and temperatures, and leave CPU fans under BIOS or firmware control where possible.

Thanks to @fejesgabortorrent-dot for requesting and testing the feature. See #26 and #27.



For older releases, see https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/releases
]]>
</CHANGES>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
old_manifest="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus.plg"
old_runtime="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus"
old_config="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus"
new_config="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2"
marker="$new_config/.migration-v1"

if [[ ! -e "$marker" ]]; then
  mkdir -p "$new_config"

  if [[ -d "$old_config" ]]; then
    for old_cfg in "$old_config"/fanctrlplus_*.cfg; do
      [[ -f "$old_cfg" ]] || continue
      old_name="${old_cfg##*/}"
      new_cfg="$new_config/fanctrlplus2_${old_name#fanctrlplus_}"
      [[ -e "$new_cfg" ]] || cp -p "$old_cfg" "$new_cfg"
    done

    if [[ -f "$old_config/order.cfg" && ! -e "$new_config/order.cfg" ]]; then
      sed 's/fanctrlplus_/fanctrlplus2_/g' "$old_config/order.cfg" > "$new_config/order.cfg.tmp" &&
        mv "$new_config/order.cfg.tmp" "$new_config/order.cfg"
    fi

    if [[ -f "$old_config/pwm_labels.cfg" && ! -e "$new_config/pwm_labels.cfg" ]]; then
      cp -p "$old_config/pwm_labels.cfg" "$new_config/pwm_labels.cfg"
    fi
  fi

  touch "$marker"
fi

if [[ -f "$old_manifest" || -d "$old_runtime" ]]; then
  echo ""
  echo "FanCtrl Plus configuration imported into FanCtrl Plus 2."
  echo "Uninstall upstream FanCtrl Plus, then install FanCtrl Plus 2 again."
  echo "Installation stopped to prevent both plugins controlling the same fans."
  echo ""
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-2026.11.12-noarch-1.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new --reinstall">
<URL>https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/releases/download/2026.11.12/fanctrlplus2-2026.11.12-noarch-1.txz</URL>
<SHA256>c224d31106be38610e3b2b8727781b2e0d4cce9a893f64e2b03e2cb6e19b5a50</SHA256>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
for package in /boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-*.txz; do
  [[ "$package" == "/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-2026.11.12-noarch-1.txz" ]] || rm -f "$package"
done

src="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/rc.fanctrlplus2"
dst="/etc/rc.d/rc.fanctrlplus2"
if [[ -f "$src" ]]; then
  ln -sf "$src" "$dst"
fi

mkdir -p /var/tmp/fanctrlplus2
# Fan alert state (faults_* directories) survives updates, so recoveries are still announced.
find /var/tmp/fanctrlplus2 -maxdepth 1 -type f -delete
pkill -f "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/array_monitor.sh"
rm -f /boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/.config.lock # the lock now lives in /var/run

# start() launches array_monitor.sh (pgrep-guarded); do not start it again here.
if [[ -x "$dst" ]]; then
  "$dst" restart
fi

echo ""
echo "------------------------------------------------"
echo " FanCtrl Plus 2 is installed."
echo " Version: 2026.11.12"
echo "------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/rc.fanctrlplus2 stop 2>/dev/null
pkill -f "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/array_monitor.sh"
removepkg fanctrlplus2 2>/dev/null || true

rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2
rm -rf /boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2
rm -f /etc/rc.d/rc.fanctrlplus2

echo "============================================================"
echo "    FanCtrl Plus 2 has been removed."
echo "    Reboot to restore fan control to BIOS or other tools."
echo "============================================================"
exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
